* NOTES

This entire document is written off the top of my head and the contents
of it are likely to change before 3.0 actually gets released.



* SYNOPSIS

Lyntin was built for the hacker/mudder.  This is a special breed of
person that's mudding and has a series of complex problems they want
to solve and mere aliases and triggers just aren't going to meet
that need.

Tintin++ has a series of commands that almost but not quite implements
(ed. bad word choice) a programming language.  Building "macros" in 
this way is complex and solves a small set of problems, but the more 
complex problems really need a full language and a library to solve.  
As such, Lyntin was built with Python and allows hackers to use Python 
to enhance Lyntin.

There a a few different methods of hacking Lyntin to suit your needs.  
They are briefly outlined here and described in more detail in their 
respective sections:

	1. editing the source code
	2. building modules
	3. command line



* EDITING THE SOURCE CODE

Lyntin is entirely written in Python.  The source code is well organized
(as of 3.0) and there will be plenty of documentation available on the
website for supporting people who want to just change the internals of
Lyntin.

FIXME - More on this at some point in time.



* BUILDING MODULES

FIXME - This is likely to change in the near future prior to the final
3.0 release.

Lyntin has a modules package and all the commands are implemented in
the 'basic' module.  You can add modules at your whim.



* COMMAND LINE

FIXME - This isn't quite implemented yet--but soon.

Lyntin allows you to execute arbitrary Python at the command line.
The code will be executed in some module--(to be determined).


